  "method": {
    "timings": "Every duration comes from a forkless byte stream: the tool is launched in a detached tmux session behind a gate FIFO, `pipe-pane` is attached before the gate is released, and bytes are timestamped as they are read. t0 is the gate release (the exec), so tmux's own spawn cost is not charged to the tool and no early byte can be missed. The predecessor polled `tmux capture-pane`, which forks tmux: measured 9.2ms p50 / 32.2ms p95 per sample, a ~22ms poll cadence and a floor under every number it published (it reported 4.2ms input latency -- below the cost of one observation).",
    "metrics": "first_byte = exec to first byte on the wire; first_paint = exec to first printable glyph (a TUI's first byte is often only cursor bookkeeping, e.g. fox's `\\e[?25l`); ttfi = exec to probe rendered, where the probe is typed as soon as the app owns the tty (termios ECHO off) and has stopped writing; key_latency = keystroke to probe rendered after a settle, with startup removed and the settle wait explicitly outside the clock.",
    "statistics": "p50 of successful launches is published, with min and p95 beside it. The predecessor published min, which hid the spread: the shipped opencode first-byte row was 101ms min against 1539ms p95. Rows with fewer than 3 successful samples are reported but not ranked.",
    "timeouts": "A timeout is recorded as a failure and excluded from the statistics, never published as a duration. The predecessor's 15s probe timeout appeared in the table as measurement (opencode 15203.1ms, amp 15053.7ms, kilo 16476.3ms) and was RANKED.",
    "probe": "Two letters not already present on the captured screen are typed; the match is made against joined bytes since the keystroke (so a cell-diffing TUI that emits the probe in pieces is still counted) AND verified against the rendered screen afterwards, so a stream false positive cannot become a number.",
    "signal": "termios ECHO is read from the pane's own pty. 'kernel-echo' means the line discipline echoed the byte before the app woke (measured: `cat` in a pty times ~0.3ms with no UI), so it describes the kernel; 'render' means the app disabled echo and repainted a cell itself. Latency is ranked within 'render' only.",
    "bundle": "the binary itself; a launcher's exec target where a wrapper hands off by path; or the package dir for script CLIs, minus any bundled node/bun runtime (weighing a 2KB shim would pretend node_modules are free; counting the interpreter would make every JS tool report the same ~140MB).",
    "memory": "PSS of the whole process tree (children included: a node CLI's workers hold real memory), floored over launches because extra processes only ever add, read after the app settles and before the latency probe perturbs it.",
    "missing": "A tool that is not installed is an n/a row and is excluded from rank counts, never estimated."
  },
